text:
Alice (Support): Hi @AcmeDev — saw your support ticket about rerank results not matching candidates. Can you paste the request body and SDK version?
AcmeDev (Jane): Hey, thanks. Using ts-sdk 2.4.1. When we call rerank the API returns 3 scores but we sent 5 candidates. Looks like scores don't line up with candidates.
AcmeDev (Jane): Minimal repro:```ts
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });
await client.rerank({
  model: 'rwd/rerank-alpha',
  input: {
    query: 'Which is most relevant?',
    candidates: [
      'Product roadmap',
      'Product roadmap\n',
      'Product roadmap\u200B',
      '\nProduct roadmap',
      'Other doc'
    ]
  }
});
```
AcmeDev (Jane): Response.body.scores.length === 3 but candidates.length === 5. We only see scores for unique-looking items; order is wrong too.
Ben (SDK Eng): thanks — that helps. @Maya any recent changes in rerank handling for array lengths?
Maya (Backend): No server-side collapse. We expect a 1:1 mapping. If the server receives a shorter array it's because the client sent a collapsed payload. Do you have a full raw request JSON?
AcmeDev (Jane): attaching raw request:```json
{"model":"rwd/rerank-alpha","input":{"query":"Which is most relevant?","candidates":["Product roadmap","Product roadmap\n","Product roadmap\u200B","\nProduct roadmap","Other doc"]}}
```
Alice (Support): Copying raw request shows the client seems to send an array, but server log shows: `received candidates object with keys: {"Product roadmap":"0","Other doc":"1"}` — that's wild.
Ben (SDK Eng): aha. Repro on our end. TS SDK 2.4.0 introduced a thin "dedupe" pipeline that builds a Map keyed by candidate.trim() to reduce token usage. When serializing we accidentally replace the candidates array with an object of unique keys -> index mapping if there are collisions. The serialization step uses `Object.fromEntries(map)` and then sends that in place of the array. On the server that becomes an object not array, and downstream code iterates object keys (in insertion order of unique keys) leading to length mismatch.
Ben (SDK Eng): Repro script:```js
const arr = ['a','a\n','\u200Ba','\na','b'];
const m = new Map();
arr.forEach((c,i)=> m.set(c.trim(), i));
const payload = { candidates: Object.fromEntries(m) };
console.log(JSON.stringify(payload));
// -> {"candidates":{"a":3,"b":4}}
```
Maya (Backend): That explains the server log. We parse `candidates` expecting Array; when it's an Object we coerce to list of keys which loses positions.
Alice (Support): @AcmeDev immediate workaround: pin to ts-sdk 2.3.x or set env RW_RERANK_DEDUPE=false (the SDK reads that flag). Also you can add zero-width marker to make each string unique (ugly workaround): `candidate + '\u200C'`
AcmeDev (Jane): Thanks — pinned to 2.3.5 and it works. Appreciate the quick help.
Ben (SDK Eng): We'll ship 2.4.2 patch to remove the buggy Object.fromEntries path and ensure we always send arrays. Patch ETA: later today. I'll add a unit test for arrays-with-close-whitespace.
Maya (Backend): On our side we'll return a 400 if `candidates` is not Array to fail-fast next time.
Alice (Support): Marking as escalated -> resolved for customer; engineering to follow-up with changelog + release notes. :thumbsup:
deploy-bot: deploy started for sdk/ts v2.4.2-hotfix -> CI passed
Ben (SDK Eng): rolling release now. please @Alice reopen if Acme sees anything odd after 2.4.2.
AcmeDev (Jane): Confirmed after pin -> behavior normal. Thanks everyone! :raised_hands:
